; A lifted lambda's parameters are classified by the LAMBDA, not by the
; function around it.
;
; Codegen keeps the parameter NAMES being emitted in word 17 and the
; declaration whose signature TYPES them in word 36. `emitFnDef` sets
; both to the same function; `emitLamDef` sets word 17 to the lambda's
; own parameters and leaves word 36 on the enclosing declaration. Until
; 2026-09-03 `curParamRefClass` read an index into the first against
; the arrow of the second, so parameter `i` of a lambda was given the
; type of parameter `i` of the function containing it.
;
; The parser curries `(lambda (a b) ...)` into `(lambda (a) (lambda (b)
; ...))`, so the inner closure record is built while word 17 is the
; OUTER lambda's parameter list - which is to say EVERY multi-parameter
; lambda took this path, and the classification was wrong whenever the
; enclosing signature disagreed at that index.
;
; It was not a wrong number. A capture classified as a reference gets
; `axiom_retain` emitted on it and its bit set in the closure record's
; shape map, so an `Int` was retained as a pointer and released as one:
; `axiom_retain` clears its `< 4096` immediate guard for any value
; above 4095 and loads from `v-16`. Term 1 below exited 139 - SIGSEGV -
; and its only difference from term 2 is the enclosing signature.
;
; What each term establishes:
;
;   1. THE CRASH. A two-parameter lambda inside `(-> String Int Int)`.
;      `a` is the lambda's first parameter and `s` is the function's,
;      so `a` was classified `String`. The literal is far above 4095
;      on purpose: a small one is swallowed by the immediate guard and
;      the bug hides.
;   2. THE CONTROL. The same lambda, same literal, inside
;      `(-> Int Int Int)`. This has always printed 1000000002; it is
;      here so that a future regression shows up as a DIFFERENCE
;      between two lines of this file rather than as one line moving.
;   3. THREE parameters, so the currying is two deep and the innermost
;      record is built two lambda bodies down from the declaration.
;   4. THE FIX MUST NOT DISARM THE REAL CASE. A lambda capturing the
;      enclosing function's genuine `String` parameter still has to
;      retain it and still has to carry its map bit - the capture is
;      named in the body, resolved through word 17 of the FUNCTION,
;      which is the pairing that was always correct. If this regresses
;      the string is freed under the closure and the length is wrong
;      or the process dies.
;   5. A lambda whose parameters SHADOW the enclosing function's, same
;      arity and same spelling. This is why the fix is a flag and not
;      a name comparison: `paramFlowBit`, `paramIsString` and
;      `paramClassOf` all guard the same pairing by comparing names,
;      and that test passes here while the bindings are still
;      different.
(import IO)

(import Str)

(:: crashed (-> String Int Int))

(fn (crashed s k)
  (let ((f 
    (lambda (a b) (+ a b))
  ))
    (+ k (f 999999999 2))
  )
)

(:: control (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (control s k)
  (let ((f 
    (lambda (a b) (+ a b))
  ))
    (+ k (f 999999999 2))
  )
)

(:: threeDeep (-> String String Int))

(fn (threeDeep s t)
  (let ((f 
    (lambda (a b c) (+ a (+ b c)))
  ))
    (f 999999997 999999998 999999999)
  )
)

(:: keepsCapture (-> String Int))

(fn (keepsCapture s)
  (let ((f 
    (lambda (n) (+ n (strLen s)))
  ))
    (f 100)
  )
)

(:: shadowed (-> String Int Int))

(fn (shadowed s k)
  (let ((f 
    (lambda (s k) (+ s k))
  ))
    (f 999999999 2)
  )
)

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  ; --- 1. the crash: a two-parameter lambda under a reference arrow ---
  {
    (println (crashed "x" 1))
    ; --- 2. the control: the same lambda under a word arrow ---
    (println (control 7 1))
    ; --- 3. three parameters, curried two deep ---
    (println (threeDeep "x" "y"))
    ; --- 4. a genuine reference capture is still counted ---
    (println (keepsCapture (strConcat "abc" "de")))
    ; --- 5. lambda parameters shadowing the function's ---
    (println (shadowed "x" 1))
    0
  }
)
